# Chapter 2: Background & Critical Review of Related Work

This chapter situates ZENITH within the landscape of recent research. Each section identifies a specific body of work, critically evaluates its contributions and limitations, and shows how ZENITH extends, combines, or reframes that work to address the governance of advanced AI.

## 2.1 Distributed and Federated AI in Space

The deployment of artificial intelligence on spacecraft has accelerated dramatically in the past five years, driven by the miniaturisation of compute hardware and the availability of high‑bandwidth inter‑satellite links. Erol et al. (2026a) provide a comprehensive survey that consolidates this emerging field into three paradigms: (i) federated learning for cross‑satellite model training with privacy‑preserving aggregation; (ii) multi‑agent algorithms for cooperative planning, resource allocation, and formation control; and (iii) collaborative sensing and distributed inference for multi‑satellite sensor fusion.

Of particular relevance to ZENITH is the *SpaceMoE* architecture, which distributes a large mixture‑of‑experts model across a constellation, with each satellite hosting a subset of experts and performing inference collaboratively. This directly addresses the size, weight, and power (SWaP) constraints that limit the computational capacity of individual satellites. By partitioning a model across multiple nodes, SpaceMoE enables constellation‑scale AI that far exceeds what any single satellite could achieve.

However, existing work focuses almost entirely on accuracy and efficiency metrics. The governance implications of distributing a model across sovereign and potentially adversarial jurisdictions remain unexplored. ZENITH extends the SpaceMoE concept by imposing a governance topology over the constellation: the seven analysis instances (Alpha–Theta) are not merely computational partitions but *epistemologically distinct* viewpoints, each governed by a different international consortium and running a fixed, non‑learning model between updates. The federation serves not only to improve accuracy but to enforce diversity—no single perspective can dominate the aggregate analysis.

Federated learning in satellite networks also faces challenges of intermittent connectivity and heterogeneous hardware. Razmi et al. (2024) demonstrate on‑board federated learning for satellite clusters using inter‑satellite links, while the LTP‑FLEO framework (Gurung & Pokhrel, 2025) addresses long‑term privacy through satellite partitioning based on predictable orbital visibility. ZENITH adopts these techniques for parameter aggregation across its seven instances, but adds a cryptographic layer: parameter updates are authenticated using session keys derived from QKD exchanges, and the aggregation protocol requires a threshold of 4‑of‑7 participating instances before a model update is accepted, providing Byzantine‑fault tolerance.

## 2.2 Energy‑Aware Scheduling for Orbital Intelligence

Low Earth orbit imposes a harsh energy discipline. A satellite at 1 200 km altitude cycles through 45 minutes of sunlight followed by 45 minutes of eclipse, during which it must rely entirely on stored energy. Poor energy management can degrade battery life by years.

Erol et al. (2026b) introduce Equinox, a decentralised runtime that frames energy‑aware scheduling as a marginal‑cost optimisation problem. Battery charge, thermal headroom, and task queue backlog are compressed into a single state‑dependent cost of execution. Tasks execute only when their marginal value exceeds this cost, enabling value‑ordered load shedding without explicit eclipse‑handling logic. Equinox demonstrates a 2.2× improvement in mean battery reserves and a 31% throughput gain over reactive baselines.

ZENITH’s orbital instances adopt Equinox as their fundamental scheduler, but extend its task‑value model to incorporate *governance priority*. Integrity‑critical operations—cryptographic attestation, anomaly reporting, kill‑switch command relay—are assigned the highest value and are therefore executed preferentially, even under severe energy constraints. Conversely, routine analysis tasks with low governance criticality are deferred or dropped. This extension ensures that the safety mechanisms of the constellation remain energetically viable during extended anomaly conditions.

## 2.3 Quantum‑Secured Space Communication

The communication links connecting the orbital constellation to Earth are a prime attack surface. An adversary capable of spoofing a NASAg command could issue a fraudulent licence revocation or, worse, a spurious kill‑switch activation. Post‑quantum cryptography (PQC) offers algorithmic resilience, but information‑theoretic security—security that holds even against unbounded computational adversaries—is desirable for the highest‑assurance channels.

Quantum Key Distribution (QKD) provides exactly this property. The Chinese Micius satellite demonstrated satellite‑to‑ground QKD in 2016, and subsequent work has focused on scaling QKD to entire constellations. Chaudhary et al. (2026) propose an end‑to‑end QKD network using twin‑field QKD and an XOR‑based key‑forwarding protocol in which intermediate satellites never possess the final secret key. This design eliminates the trusted‑node problem that plagues earlier QKD network architectures. Vergoossen et al. (2019) develop trusted‑node QKD constellations with key buffers, while Gao et al. (2025) optimise global quantum communication via constellation topology design.

ZENITH integrates twin‑field QKD for the ground‑to‑orbit uplink carrying NASAg commands. The downlink, which conveys only public spectrum analysis, does not require secrecy, but its integrity is protected by quantum‑secure authentication codes derived from the same key material. For inter‑satellite links, QKD is used to establish session keys that protect federated‑learning parameter exchanges against eavesdropping and tampering. Hossain et al. (2025) address opportunistic scheduling for QKD in single‑downlink scenarios, a technique directly applicable to ZENITH’s pass‑based key generation schedule.

The combination of QKD with post‑quantum cryptography (Kyber/Dilithium) provides defence‑in‑depth: the QKD layer guarantees information‑theoretic security for key exchange during operational windows, while PQC algorithms serve as a fallback when QKD links are unavailable.

## 2.4 Autonomous Satellite Health and Fault Management

A constellation of 84 satellites requires autonomous fault management to maintain availability without overwhelming ground operators. Traditional approaches rely on telemetry thresholding, but subtle faults—such as clock phase jumps or gradual thermal degradation—are often missed until they cause mission‑affecting anomalies.

Iiyama et al. (2024) propose a rigidity‑based fault detection method for satellite constellations using inter‑satellite link measurements. Satellites are modelled as vertices of a graph, and faults are identified through singular values of geometric‑centred Euclidean distance matrices (GCEDM). The method does not require precise ephemeris data, making it suitable for constellations with frequent orbital adjustments.

Lei et al. (2026) extend inter‑satellite ranging to autonomous clock fault monitoring, demonstrating that dual one‑way inter‑satellite links can detect clock phase jumps with high sensitivity. This is critical for ZENITH, where cryptographic attestation depends on accurate timestamps and clock drift could be exploited for replay attacks.

For anomaly detection in telemetry streams, Huo et al. (2024) present a self‑supervised approach based on Physics‑Informed Real NVP neural networks, while He et al. (2022) apply sparse feature extraction with one‑class SVM to satellite telemetry. Mullan et al. (2024) demonstrate baseline health monitoring using the EIRSAT‑1 educational satellite, providing a template for small‑satellite autonomous health management.

ZENITH combines these techniques into a three‑layer monitoring architecture: (1) supervised baseline methods flag known fault signatures; (2) unsupervised clustering (sparse feature‑based SVM) detects novel anomalies without a pre‑existing database; and (3) a physics‑informed neural network provides interpretability for root‑cause analysis. Importantly, ZENITH extends the fault‑detection domain to include *governance faults*—deviations in an instance’s analytical behaviour that may indicate compromise rather than hardware failure. Detection of such faults triggers cryptographic attestation requests and may lead to automatic isolation of the suspect instance from the federation.

## 2.5 Formal Verification of AI Containment Infrastructure

The containment of agentic AI systems requires infrastructure that is itself free of exploitable vulnerabilities. The April 2026 frontier‑model escape (Blain, 2026a) demonstrated that sandbox escapes can succeed by exploiting subtle integer‑overflow patterns in C/C++ code that are missed by fuzzing and static analysis. In response, two complementary formal verification approaches have emerged.

*COBALT* (Blain, 2026a) is a Z3 SMT‑based engine that targets CWE‑190/191/195 arithmetic vulnerability patterns. It translates C/C++ source into SMT constraints, queries the solver for satisfiability, and returns either a concrete witness (demonstrating a vulnerability) or a UNSAT verdict (proving absence of vulnerabilities within specified bounds). Blain & Noiseux (2026) applied COBALT to 3 500 code artefacts from seven LLMs and found that standard industry tools missed 97.8 % of the Z3‑proven vulnerabilities.

Mitchell (2026) proposes *containment verification*—a methodology that formally verifies the agentic framework software rather than the AI model itself. By treating the model as an unconstrained “havoc oracle,” containment verification proves that the containment layer will enforce safety policies for every possible AI output, independently of the model’s future capabilities. This provides a guarantee that is invariant to model capability growth.

ZENITH adopts both approaches. The firmware of the IAGH TEE 3.2 security co‑processor (ARM Cortex‑M85 code controlling FLOP limits, the kill‑switch, and licence validation) is subjected to COBALT analysis before deployment. Any SAT verdict blocks the release; only UNSAT‑verified firmware is permitted. The agentic framework that mediates between the terrestrial AI agent’s outputs and the external world undergoes containment verification, ensuring that even if the AI proposes a policy‑violating action, the containment layer prevents its execution. These verification activities are mandated by NASAg certification Level 3 and above.

## 2.6 Hardware‑Level Governance and Cryptographic Off‑Switches

Software‑enforced policies have a fundamental weakness: they can be disabled by any party with sufficient system privilege. Hardware‑level governance mechanisms address this by embedding policy enforcement into the silicon itself.

Petrie (2025) proposes an *embedded off‑switch* design in which thousands of independent security blocks are interspersed throughout an AI accelerator’s die. Each block uses public‑key cryptography to verify a licence nonce and controls a local power gate. The attacker must physically disable or cryptographically forge thousands of checks simultaneously to circumvent the off‑switch. The design is compatible with standard CMOS fabrication processes and adds less than 2 % area overhead.

Ansari et al. (2026) present a taxonomy of 20 hardware‑level governance mechanisms, evaluating their technical feasibility for regulatory compliance and treaty verification. They identify a structural mismatch: the mechanisms most needed for treaty verification—on‑chip compute metering, cryptographic proof‑of‑training, and hardware‑embedded enforcement—are the least mature, while mature mechanisms address lower‑assurance compliance needs. The flexHEG (flexible Hardware‑Enabled Guarantees) architecture embeds a dedicated “Guarantee Processor” within a tamper‑evident secure enclosure to bridge this gap.

ZENITH’s IAGH TEE 3.2 specification is directly informed by these proposals. Each compliant terrestrial agent incorporates the embedded off‑switch design, a Guarantee Processor for policy enforcement, and on‑chip compute metering that provides cryptographically signed telemetry to NASAg. The licence validation path involves no software at all: the licence nonce is presented directly to the security blocks via a hardware interface, and the power gates are controlled by analogue switches that cannot be overridden by any code executing on the main processor.

## 2.7 International Treaty Frameworks and Compute Governance

The idea of governing AI through control of the computational resources required to train it has gained significant traction. Ramamoorthy et al. (2024) propose a global compute cap treaty that would ban training runs above an agreed‑upon FLOP threshold, establish an international agency to enforce it, and create emergency response protocols for suspected violations. Cihon et al. (2025) develop four institutional models: (i) domestic frontier AI regulation, (ii) an IAEA‑for‑AI (International AI Agency), (iii) an NPT‑for‑AI (Secure Chips Agreement), and (iv) a US‑led Allied Public‑Private Partnership. Anderljung et al. (2025) review existing international safety agreements and recommend a conditional AI safety treaty with compute thresholds, mandatory audits, and a network of AI Safety Institutes.

These proposals have established the conceptual foundations of compute governance, but they have largely treated the enforcement mechanism as a black box to be specified later. ZENITH can be seen as the *black‑box opener*: it provides the specific hardware, cryptographic, and institutional mechanisms that would operationalise a compute‑cap treaty. The NASAg authority is the IAEA‑for‑AI. The IAGH TEE 3.2 and its embedded off‑switches are the on‑chip verification mechanism. The CRL‑based licence revocation process is the enforcement lever. The public transparency registry satisfies the audit requirements.

Crucially, ZENITH’s hardware‑enforcement model makes the treaty verifiable at the physical level. A nation that signs the treaty but later attempts to train a frontier model in secret would need to either fabricate custom AI accelerators without the required security blocks (detectable through supply‑chain monitoring) or physically disable thousands of on‑chip security features (detectable through forensic analysis of seized hardware). This is qualitatively different from verifying compliance with software‑only restrictions, which can be circumvented by a single rogue system administrator.

## 2.8 Space Sustainability and Orbital Debris Mitigation

The deployment of an 84‑satellite constellation carries environmental responsibilities. Bennett (2025) argues that orbital debris requires prevention and mitigation across the entire satellite life cycle, including stronger regulations, fiscal interventions, multilateral institutions, and active debris removal. Bastida Virgili et al. (2023) introduce OPUS, an integrated assessment model that combines astrodynamic simulations with economic behaviour modelling to evaluate orbital sustainability policies. Their findings indicate that near‑term orbital sustainability is controlled primarily by three variables: disposal reliability for newly launched spacecraft, encounter‑state uncertainty in high‑risk conjunction events, and the residual hazard posed by inactive high‑mass legacy objects.

Turyshev (2026) models the stability, control, and market formation dynamics of orbital debris, providing economic metrics for evaluating constellation‑level sustainability. Bauer et al. (2025) model orbital decay due to atmospheric drag, informing end‑of‑life deorbit strategies.

ZENITH incorporates sustainability as a first‑order design constraint. Every satellite is equipped with sufficient propellant for a controlled deorbit within 25 years (meeting current international guidelines) and is designed for passivation—all stored energy is depleted before final disposal to prevent explosion‑generated debris. The solid‑state design (no moving parts, flywheels for energy storage) minimises the risk of catastrophic fragmentation during collision. The constellation’s 1 200 km altitude places it above the densest debris bands but below the geostationary arc, simplifying end‑of‑life disposal. An OPUS‑based lifecycle assessment, conducted as part of the operational plan, will be updated quadrennially and published as part of the public transparency mandate.

## 2.9 Synthesis of Research Gaps

The literature reveals a series of discrete but unconnected advances: federated learning in space works but lacks a governance topology; QKD networks provide information‑theoretic security but are not yet integrated into a command‑and‑control architecture for AI governance; formal verification tools can find sandbox vulnerabilities but have not been mandated as part of a hardware‑certification regime; hardware off‑switches exist on paper but have not been embedded in a comprehensive licensing framework; compute‑governance treaties are conceptually promising but lack a concrete enforcement back‑end.

ZENITH’s contribution is *architectural synthesis*. It takes these disparate lines of research and weaves them into a single, coherent system in which the output of each component becomes the input to the next: the QKD‑secured channel delivers licences to the hardware off‑switches; the off‑switches enforce the FLOP ceiling that realises the compute cap; the violation response cascade provides graduated enforcement; the formally verified TEE ensures that no software exploit can bypass the hardware; the federated learning instances provide spectrum analysis that prevents interpretive monopoly; and the NASAg governance structure legitimises the entire edifice. The system’s safety derives not from any single mechanism but from the multiplication of independent constraints, each anchored in a different physical or cryptographic substrate.

