The ZENITH architecture rests on the claim that physical separation, hardware‑enforced constraints, and mandatory analytic diversity can provide existential safety guarantees that software‑only approaches cannot. To substantiate this claim, the present chapter develops a formal mathematical framework. We define the Three Sacred Separations as partial orders on capability domains, prove that they are orthogonal and individually necessary, formalise the Spectrum Analysis protocol and its resilience properties, derive the Contextual Incompleteness theorems that preserve human agency, model hardware constraints as physical invariants, and finally establish a set of multi‑layered safety theorems that compose these elements into a redundant safety architecture. Where the proofs rely on properties of physical implementations, we reference the concrete hardware mechanisms surveyed in Chapter 2 (Ansari et al., 2026; Petrie, 2025) and detailed in Chapter 6.

