### 3.4 Hardware Constraint Mathematics

The separation principles are given physical teeth by the IAGH TEE 3.2 hardware. We model the key enforcement mechanisms as formal invariants.

#### 3.4.1 FLOP Ceiling Invariant

Let \( L_{\text{comp}} \) be the licensed FLOP rate (operations per second). The actual instantaneous FLOP rate at time \( t \) is \( f(t) \), measured by cycle‑accurate hardware counters.

**Invariant 3.1 (FLOP Ceiling).** For any terrestrial agent operating under a valid licence,
\[
\int_{0}^{T} f(\tau)\, d\tau \le L_{\text{comp}} \cdot T \quad \forall T \in [0, \text{licence\_expiry}].
\]

The enforcement is achieved by an analogue current‑shunt measurement and a MOSFET power gate that throttles the main processor clock when the cumulative integral approaches the limit within a 5% tolerance band (Petrie, 2025). Software cannot disable the current measurement because it is wired in series with the processor’s power supply; any attempt to bypass it would require physical modification that triggers tamper detection.

**Theorem 3.5 (Unbypassable Ceiling).**  
No sequence of software instructions executed solely on the main processor can cause the inequality in Invariant 3.1 to be violated. The ceiling is a physical law of the device, not a policy.

*Proof.* (By contradiction.) Assume a software exploit \( E \) exists that causes \( f(t) \) to exceed the licensed rate without triggering throttling. Then \( E \) must either (i) falsify the current measurement, (ii) open the power gate without authorisation, or (iii) execute floating‑point operations without drawing current. (i) The measurement shunt is an analogue component whose output is digitised inside the isolated security co‑processor; software on the main processor has no access to the digitisation path. (ii) The power gate is controlled by a dedicated GPIO of the security co‑processor; the main processor cannot toggle this GPIO. (iii) Executing a floating‑point operation requires current flow through the processor; the shunt measures this current. Therefore, none of the three options is feasible within the defined threat model. This contradiction establishes the theorem. □

#### 3.4.2 Memory Bandwidth Limiter

The token‑bucket mechanism for memory bandwidth is modelled as follows. Let \( B_{\text{lic}} \) be the licensed bandwidth (bytes/second). A bucket has capacity \( C \) and is refilled at rate \( r = B_{\text{lic}} \). At time \( t \), the bucket level is \( b(t) \). A memory request of size \( s \) consumes \( s \) tokens if available; otherwise it is stalled.

**Invariant 3.2 (Memory Bandwidth).** For any interval \( \Delta t \),
\[
\text{bytes\_transferred}(\Delta t) \le r\Delta t + C.
\]

Because the bucket is implemented in the memory controller hardware, no software path exists to bypass the token check. This is a standard traffic‑shaping result (Bertsekas & Gallager, 1992) adapted to the hardware context.

#### 3.4.3 PUF Entropy and Key Derivation

The Physically Unclonable Function (PUF) provides the root of trust. We require that the PUF output distribution has min‑entropy \( H_{\infty} \ge 128 \) bits, as verified over 10 000 challenges across temperature and voltage corners (NIST SP 800‑90B methodology). The device unique key is derived via a strong extractor:
\[
K_{\text{DUK}} = \text{HKDF‑SHA3‑512}(\text{PUF\_resp}, \text{salt}).
\]
Under the assumption that the PUF response is unpredictable, \( K_{\text{DUK}} \) is computationally indistinguishable from random and cannot be cloned or extracted without destroying the device.

