## 10.4 Cost Analysis and Economic Sustainability

The total Phase 1‑4 capital cost is estimated at $32 B, with an annual operational budget of $5 B by Phase 4. These figures are comparable to other large‑scale space infrastructure programmes—the Galileo navigation system (~€10 B), Starlink’s deployment cost (~$10 B for the first 4 000 satellites, though with a simpler payload), and the ITER fusion reactor (~$25 B). Unlike those programmes, ZENITH generates a recurring revenue stream through licensing fees and analysis services, which can offset operational costs once the constellation is operational.

**Licensing revenue model.** C1‑class devices (the vast majority of the installed base) are licensed at near‑zero cost to encourage broad adoption. C2 and C3 devices are charged an annual licence fee calibrated to recover the marginal cost of the compliance monitoring infrastructure. ME‑Class licences carry a substantial fee—on the order of 0.1 % of the estimated training cost—that contributes to the constellation’s operational budget. At projected ME‑Class training frequencies (1–5 runs per year globally), this fee stream alone could cover 20–30 % of annual operational costs.

**Orbital analysis service fees.** Access to the spectrum analysis layer is free for public‑interest queries and for licensed research institutions. Commercial entities seeking priority access, higher throughput, or custom analysis configurations pay a tiered subscription fee. This model is analogous to that of the European Centre for Medium‑Range Weather Forecasts (ECMWF), which provides basic weather data freely while charging for high‑resolution and custom products.

**Cost‑reduction trajectory.** Satellite manufacturing costs are expected to decline by 20–30 % per doubling of constellation size, following the learning‑curve effects observed in commercial constellations. The use of commercial‑off‑the‑shelf components (where radiation requirements permit) and standardised satellite buses further reduces cost. The Phase 4 second‑generation satellites are projected to cost 50–60 % of the Phase 3 units per unit of compute capacity.

**Economic sustainability under non‑participation.** If a major AI‑producing nation remains outside the ZENITH framework, the loss of licensing revenue from that nation is partially offset by the increased value of the orbital analysis layer to participating states, who gain an informational advantage. The economic analysis in Appendix D models several participation scenarios and concludes that ZENITH is economically sustainable with as few as 15 participating high‑income nations, provided those nations collectively represent at least 40 % of global AI compute demand.

## 10.5 Integration Path with Existing Space and AI Infrastructure

ZENITH is designed to integrate with, rather than replace, existing infrastructure.

**Launch integration.** The satellite mass and volume are compatible with the payload fairings of Falcon 9, Falcon Heavy, Ariane 6, and H3 launch vehicles. ZENITH can therefore procure launch services competitively, avoiding dependence on any single provider. The 12‑satellite‑per‑plane configuration allows a single Falcon 9 to launch an entire plane’s primary satellites, reducing campaign complexity.

**Ground segment integration.** The optical ground stations are co‑located with existing astronomical observatories and space‑communication facilities, taking advantage of established atmospheric monitoring, adaptive optics, and fibre‑optic backhaul. The NASAg telemetry ingestion pipeline runs on standard cloud infrastructure (AWS, Azure, GCP), with the cryptographic validation performed on dedicated HSMs to maintain the chain of trust.

**AI ecosystem integration.** The IAGH TEE 3.2 specification is published as an open standard under a royalty‑free licence. Any semiconductor manufacturer may implement it, and the reference design (RISC‑V‑based) is provided to lower the barrier to entry. The goal is to make IAGH compatibility a market norm, much as Wi‑Fi certification or hardware security modules have become standard features. The offline licensing firmware pilot (Section 10.2.1) demonstrates that existing hardware can be retrofitted, providing an immediate on‑ramp for data centres that wish to participate before IAGH‑native silicon is available.

**Space sustainability integration.** ZENITH’s debris mitigation measures (Chapter 9) are filed with the United Nations Office for Outer Space Affairs (UNOOSA) as part of the constellation’s registration. The constellation’s ephemerides are publicly shared via the Space‑Track.org catalogue, and the collision avoidance system uses the standard Conjunction Data Messages (CDMs) format, allowing seamless coordination with other operators.

