# Appendix D – Simulated Constellation Performance Data

## D.1 Simulation Methodology

We constructed a simulation of the ZENITH orbital constellation using the Systems Tool Kit (STK) Astrogator module with high‑precision ephemerides. The constellation comprised 84 active satellites in 7 sun‑synchronous orbital planes at 1 200 km altitude, inclination 97.4°, with 12 satellites per plane evenly spaced in true anomaly. Two on‑orbit spares per plane were included but not used in baseline performance metrics. Ground segment included 12 optical/Ka‑band stations distributed globally at NASA Deep Space Network and ESA ESTRACK sites. Simulations ran for a 30‑day period with 1‑second time steps, covering all seasons.

Key performance metrics were evaluated:

- Ground coverage statistics
- Inter‑satellite link (ISL) connectivity
- Latency distribution
- Data throughput
- Energy availability
- Redundancy and fault tolerance

## D.2 Coverage and Visibility

Table D.1 summarises the global coverage results.

| Metric                          | Value                     |
|---------------------------------|---------------------------|
| Minimum satellites in view (any location) | 2                  |
| Maximum satellites in view     | 6                         |
| Mean satellites in view         | 3.8                       |
| Percentage of time ≥4 satellites visible at 50°N | 98.2% |
| Maximum gap in coverage (any ground point) | 47 seconds |
| Mean revisit time (same satellite) | 4.2 hours              |

The constellation provides robust, multi‑fold coverage worldwide, ensuring that any terrestrial query can be served with redundancy. The brief coverage gaps occur only at extreme polar latitudes (>80°) and are mitigated by inter‑satellite routing of queued queries.

## D.3 Inter‑Satellite Link Connectivity

Each satellite maintains laser ISLs with its four nearest neighbours (two intra‑plane, two inter‑plane). The mesh topology was evaluated for connectivity and path diversity.

| Metric                          | Value                     |
|---------------------------------|---------------------------|
| Average ISL link availability (clear line of sight) | 99.7% |
| Mean number of active neighbours per satellite | 3.92 |
| Maximum end‑to‑end hops between any two satellites | 7 |
| Average path length (shortest path) | 3.1 hops |
| ISL data rate per link          | 100 Gbps (6 channels)    |
| Total constellation cross‑section bandwidth | 16.8 Tbps (84 × 4 × 100 Gbps / 2) |

The mesh retains full connectivity even with the loss of up to 3 satellites per plane, as verified by Monte Carlo failure injection.

## D.4 Latency Analysis

Figure D.1 (not reproduced here) shows the cumulative distribution function of round‑trip latency from ground station to satellite and back, including the 100 ms deliberate buffering. The results are consistent with Theorem 3.1.

| Percentile | Round‑Trip Latency (ms) |
|------------|-------------------------|
| 5th        | 102                     |
| 25th       | 104                     |
| 50th (median) | 106                  |
| 75th       | 111                     |
| 95th       | 127                     |
| 99th       | 145                     |

The minimum latency of ~102 ms confirms that real‑time control loops (<10 ms) are physically impossible. The deliberate buffering and batch processing account for the additional delays over the theoretical propagation time.

## D.5 Data Throughput and Queue Analysis

The downlink bandwidth is limited to 1 Gbps aggregate across the constellation. Assuming a typical query rate of 10 000 per second and a response package size of 50 KB (including seven instances and meta‑analysis), the required downlink capacity is 4 Gbps. To stay within the 1 Gbps limit, responses are prioritised and queued. The simulation shows:

| Metric                          | Value                     |
|---------------------------------|---------------------------|
| Mean queue length per satellite | 12.4 responses           |
| 95th percentile queue wait time | 310 ms                   |
| Maximum queue wait time (1‑hour simulation) | 1.2 s          |
| Throughput achieved              | 998 Mbps (99.8% of allocated) |

The Equinox scheduler (Section 5.3) ensures that governance‑critical responses are prioritised, so that emergency telemetry and licence commands never experience queue delay beyond the physical latency.

## D.6 Energy Balance

Each satellite’s energy state was simulated using a detailed power model. The Equinox marginal‑cost scheduling kept the flywheel state of charge (SoC) within safe limits.

| Metric                          | Value                     |
|---------------------------------|---------------------------|
| Minimum SoC during any eclipse  | 62% (safe margin)        |
| Average SoC at end of eclipse   | 78%                       |
| Number of eclipses where SoC dropped below 50% | 0 (over 30 days) |
| Flywheel cycle depth (average)  | 18% (out of 100–0% usable) |

The 15 kWh flywheel and efficient scheduling provide ample margin, with the 2 kWh LTO emergency battery never activated during the nominal simulation.

## D.7 Fault Injection and Resilience

We injected random satellite failures (instant loss of all functions) at a rate of one per week to assess constellation resilience. The rigidity‑based fault detection (Section 5.5) identified failures with a mean time to detection of 8.3 seconds and no false positives. After failure, the constellation rebalanced the federated learning loads within 120 seconds. No query was lost; 99.97% were processed within 500 ms of the normal latency distribution, thanks to ISL rerouting and spare activation.

These simulated results validate the architectural claims of high availability, robust security margins, and graceful degradation. Full simulation source code and raw data are available in the dissertation repository.

