# Appendix E – Pilot Study Protocols & Ethical Clearances

## E.1 Overview

Three pilot studies were designed to retire critical risks before full ZENITH deployment (Section 10.2). This appendix provides the detailed protocols and documentation of ethical review.

## E.2 Pilot 1: Offline Licensing Firmware on NVIDIA H100 Systems

**Objective:** Validate licence acquisition, enforcement, and revocation on production GPU hardware without custom silicon.

**Study Design:**
- **Hardware:** 100 NVIDIA H100 GPUs across three cloud data centres (AWS us‑east‑1, Azure westeurope, GCP asia‑southeast1).
- **Software:** BMC firmware agent implementing the full CBOR licence stack (Appendix C) and power‑based FLOP estimation.
- **Duration:** 6 months of continuous operation, including 2 weeks of dedicated red‑team attacks.
- **Data collection:** Telemetry (power, estimated FLOPs, violation events, licence transactions) streamed every 5 minutes to a test NASAg server; monthly operator surveys on usability.

**Ethical Clearance:**
- Institutional Review Board (IRB) approval obtained from [University Ethics Committee] (protocol #ZENITH‑2026‑001).
- No personally identifiable information (PII) collected; only device‑level metrics.
- Participating data centre operators signed a Data Use Agreement (DUA) covering confidentiality and data handling.
- Red‑team attacks were conducted in isolated sandbox environments, ensuring no impact on production workloads.
- The study was classified as minimal risk; no human subjects research beyond voluntary operator surveys (informed consent obtained).

**Pilot Status:** Active; preliminary results reported in Section 10.2.1.

## E.3 Pilot 2: Single‑Satellite Federated Learning Test (Phase 2, 2028–2029)

**Objective:** Demonstrate federated learning convergence under orbital dynamics and energy constraints.

**Study Design:**
- **Space Segment:** Two ZENITH‑ORB prototype satellites (Section 10.2.2) in 1 200 km sun‑synchronous orbit, each carrying a 64‑core RISC‑V cluster with NPU.
- **Ground Segment:** Primary ground station at [Location]; backup at [Location]. Optical feeder link for high‑rate data.
- **Task:** Supervised learning for ship detection from synthetic aperture radar (SAR) data, a non‑military, environmental monitoring use case.
- **Data:** Open‑source SAR datasets (Sentinel‑1), partitioned across satellites to simulate heterogeneous local data.
- **Protocol:** Federated averaging with secure aggregation (LTP‑FLEO), synchronised every 15 minutes during mutual visibility. Equinox scheduler manages energy.
- **Success Criteria:**
  - Federated model accuracy within 5% of centralised baseline after 100 rounds.
  - Mean satellite battery SoC >70%.
  - Zero successful gradient inversion attacks (privacy audit by independent lab).

**Ethical Clearance:**
- Space mission review by [National Space Agency] and UNOOSA registration.
- IRB approval [University] (protocol #ZENITH‑2028‑003) for data handling and privacy evaluation.
- Environmental assessment: Satellite design complies with IADC debris mitigation guidelines; deorbit plan filed.
- The SAR data contain no PII; ship detection does not identify individual vessels. Use of Sentinel‑1 data follows ESA’s free and open data policy.
- No weapons or military applications involved.

**Pilot Status:** Planned for Q3 2028 launch.

## E.4 Pilot 3: QKD Network End‑to‑End Demo (2027–2028)

**Objective:** Establish end‑to‑end quantum‑secured key exchange between two ground stations via a LEO satellite relay.

**Study Design:**
- **Satellite:** A commercial small satellite (e.g., Spire Global cubesat) modified to carry a weak‑coherent‑pulse QKD source (1550 nm) and an on‑board key‑forwarding processor running the XOR protocol.
- **Ground Stations:** Two optical ground stations (Observatories A and B, separation ~1 200 km). Each equipped with superconducting nanowire detectors and adaptive optics.
- **Key Generation:** Twin‑field QKD during satellite passes (~5 minutes per pass, 4 passes per day per station).
- **Key Usage:** Generated keys used to authenticate simulated NASAg licence revocation commands to a test IAGH TEE 3.2 device (ground lab).
- **Success Criteria:**
  - Secret key rate ≥1 kbps sustained over a pass.
  - QBER <5% with no evidence of eavesdropping.
  - End‑to‑end key relay successful; intermediate satellite logs confirm no access to final key.
  - Simulated command correctly executed by test device within 2 seconds.

**Ethical Clearance:**
- IRB exemption (no human subjects).
- Laser safety: Ground stations comply with ANSI Z136.1 and IEC 60825‑1; satellite laser within eye‑safe limits at ground.
- Radio frequency spectrum coordination with ITU and national regulators.
- QKD experiment does not interfere with other satellite services.
- Data generated (key rates, QBER) are non‑sensitive and will be published openly.

**Pilot Status:** Satellite integration underway; target launch Q1 2028.

## E.5 Informed Consent and Data Management

For all pilots, any human participant (e.g., operators) provided informed consent after receiving a plain‑language description of the study, data collected, and rights to withdraw. All telemetry and pilot data are stored on encrypted, access‑controlled servers and retained for 10 years post‑study. Anonymised data will be released under Creative Commons CC‑BY‑4.0 after an embargo period.

The ethical protocols described here align with the principles of the Belmont Report and the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), ensuring that ZENITH’s development respects human subjects and public values.
